import{_ as n,P as i,d as a,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const v=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.4 Generative Node (HV04)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.4 Generative Node (HV04)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1117,"xkLessons":{"A.4":{"title":"A.4 Generative Node (HV04)","lead":"The variable “Generative Node” separates generative conditions, actors, and events. An unidentified actor does not mean an absence of actors has been proved; the fact of generation creates neither justification, complete responsibility, nor continuing authority.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4034–4117; original site location /theory/appendix-a/a-04#section-a-4. For the current full text, see the theory-v2 source; historical research manuscripts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","Brief quotation: “Generation must be described as three separate items: the generative condition (§7.2.1), the generative subject (§7.2.2), and the generative event (§7.2.3).” The generative condition concerns how conditions and constraints permit new states to form; the generative subject denotes acting subjects with corresponding capabilities, roles, and standing; the generative event denotes detectable new states or transitions. The three may be connected but cannot replace one another. An event occurring does not mean an actor has been identified, and a visible person does not mean that person created the entire result. [T-main; T-types]","Route one (sorting into categories) permits separately recording the candidate generative condition, generative subject, and generative event, and the state of an unidentified subject, with conclusions stopping at the distinction of categories. Listing no inferential premises does not mean evidence is unnecessary: the conditions for evidence, the conditions for sources, and specific object material remain required. Successful classification also does not establish a causal generation mechanism among the three. [T-main]","The source text permits emergent generation without an identifiable actor, but “currently unidentified” is not “proved to have no actor.” Observed local interactions and overall changes can be objects of bridging research. A claim about an actorless mechanism additionally requires corresponding channel and comparison evidence; an unknown initiator cannot be concealed behind “natural emergence.” [T-main]","Route two (generative mechanism) requires a pre-registered channel-effect test identifying an above-threshold effect of the generative condition, the generative subject, or an actorless emergent channel on a specified state transition of the generative event, and further requires the conditions for causal claims and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals.” What can be concluded is a candidate generation mechanism and its typing within specified scales, time windows, and channels. It does not automatically establish that any technology has intentions, that any crisis wants to change institutions, or that the most visible person bears all responsibility. [T-main; T-G2]","State nine-axis scales and times for the generative condition, generative subject, and generative event separately: condition accumulation, actor action, event timing, and diffusion delays may differ. Generative subjects, conditions, and events may change with scale. Moving across levels requires new units and populations, representation relations, responsibility types, and external impacts; individual initiation cannot directly establish the intention of an organization as a whole. [T-main]","The fact of generation establishes neither justification nor complete responsibility; having initiated something does not grant an actor permanent continuing authorization. Outputs may establish uptake needs, descriptions of state transitions, and a starting point for a responsibility chain, but a “starting point” is not a final attribution of responsibility. Starting, spreading, or stopping any real-world process requires independent choice and authorization; the variable does not issue orders itself. [T-main]","Those classified as actors may safely challenge attributed intentions, roles, and authorization, with anti-retaliation protection, and request review outside the original typing chain. When restoration is authorized, correct the categories of generative condition, generative subject, and generative event, actually remove erroneous attributions of intention/responsibility and downstream effects, leave a record of the changes, and verify completion. Merely renaming “responsible person” as “generator” while continuing to affect evaluations is insufficient. [T-main]","The SEC's 2013 order on the 2012 Knight incident separately records omissions in code deployment, activation of an old program, and order outputs. This can help record technical conditions, personnel conduct, and event outcomes separately. The technical chain does not make a program an intentional actor, and the order's regulatory findings cannot be substituted in this explanation for a complete responsibility judgment about any individual. [P-KNIGHT]","Item-by-item study (“Source text” below retains the item’s complete content; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Code. The source text gives the code only in parentheses at the end of the heading and uses the name in the text. Interpretation: The code is a short identifier within the code system of Appendix B, tracking the same variable; it does not mean a real object has been proved. [T-main]","02 | Qualified code. The source text does not list this item separately. Interpretation: The qualified form serves only to avoid name collisions; references to this variable must still carry the object and evidence status. [T-main]","03 | Name. Source heading: Generative Node. Interpretation: The name provides an entry point for reading, not an automatic label for a particular person, group, or real structure. [T-main]","04 | Proposition role. The source text does not list this item separately; under §2.2 it belongs to the role of “human-domain clauses.” Interpretation: this marks it as a proposition of the human domain; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Role in the system. The source text lists it as one of the eleven human variables of Part 7 (§7.3). Interpretation: this is the definition of a human variable, specifying how it enters analysis; listing the items does not establish that any empirical test holds. [T-main]","06 | Proposition. Source text: Generation must be described as three separate items: the generative condition (§7.2.1), the generative subject (§7.2.2), and the generative event (§7.2.3). Emergent generation can occur without an identifiable subject. Interpretation: The generative condition, generative subject, and generative event are distinct categories. Emergence without an identifiable actor is permitted, but an unknown-actor state cannot directly be upgraded to proved absence of an actor. [T-main]","07 | Scope of application. Source text: Formation of new actions, organizations, institutions, or state transitions in human structures. Interpretation: The scope lists task domains that may be examined; it does not mean every object within them has empirical support. [T-main]","08 | Conditions for suspension. Source text: Suspend the relevant claims about the subject or the type when a condition is personified, an event is treated as a subject, or standing as a subject has not been confirmed on any grounds. Route one may still record the candidate generative condition, generative subject, and generative event separately, along with the status “subject not yet identified.” A subject not yet identified is not taken as proof that there is none. Interpretation: When personification is erroneous or actor standing insufficient, pause the corresponding assertions. Route one’s candidate conditions, actors, events, and unidentified states may still remain separately. [T-main]","09 | Inferential premises. Source text: None. Interpretation: This explicitly says “none,” with no inferential premises listed. It does not waive the conditions for sources and the conditions for evidence, or turn route one’s typing into causal proof. [T-main]","10 | Method requirements. Source text: Conditions for evidence (§2.5); conditions for sources (§2.7). Interpretation: Method requirements constrain evidence collection and expression; meeting method requirements does not independently establish the truth of empirical propositions. [T-main]","11 | Domain restriction. Source text: None. Interpretation: writing “none” means only that no such domain restriction is listed, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a listed restriction also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Objects of application. Source text: None. Interpretation: objects of application cannot replace the current object’s definition; writing “none” is an explicit statement, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Support routes. Source text: Route one, sorting into categories. Conclusion level: Descriptive classification. Conditions: The items required for the generative condition, the generative subject, and the generative event can each be stated separately, and the two statuses “emergence without a subject” and “subject not yet identified” are retained. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Record a candidate generative condition, a candidate generative subject, and a candidate generative event separately. The three do not substitute for one another. Ceiling: Distinction by category only. The existence of a condition, subject, or event does not establish either of the other two or a causal mechanism of generation. Route two, generative mechanism. Conclusion level: Mechanism explanation. Conditions: A pre-registered channel-effect test (§4.4) identifies an above-threshold effect of the generative condition, the generative subject, or a channel of emergence without a subject on the state transition of the specified generative event. Further inferential premises: This channel-effect test. Further method requirements: Conditions for causal claims (§2.4); Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals (§4.8.4). Can conclude: Record a candidate mechanism of generation within the specified scale, time window, and channel, and its classification by generative condition, generative subject, and generative event. Ceiling: Conditions must not be personified, a subject must not be inferred backward from an event, and normative justification, responsibility, or authorization must not be inferred from the fact of generation. Interpretation: Route one only classifies. Route two requires a pre-registered channel-effect test to identify the effect of the generative condition, the generative subject, or an actorless channel on a specified generative event, and further requires the conditions for causal claims and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals”; an event having occurred does not justify inferring an actor backward. [T-main]","14 | Can infer. Source text: Conditional paths of generation; generation with or without a subject. Interpretation: These are permitted directions of conclusion; which is actually reached, and at what strength, remains bounded by the selected route and material. [T-main]","15 | Cannot infer. Source text: That a technology or a crisis has intent; that a generative subject automatically has continuing authorization. Interpretation: These are common boundary crossings that cannot be avoided merely by rephrasing; examine actual inferences and subsequent use, not just wording. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale. Source text: Aggregation level covers units of exposure to conditions, of action by subjects, and of generative events, with their respective populations of cases, distributions, and aggregation rules. Spatial extent covers sites of generation, organizational or platform boundaries, areas of diffusion, and cross-domain environments. Time span covers the period of accumulation of conditions, the window of the subject’s action, the time of the event, and diffusion lag. Organizational level covers roles in action, generative teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. Causal level covers generative events, mechanisms of interaction between subjects and conditions, meso-level structures of generation, institutional arrangements, and systemic conditions. Observation resolution covers the original conditions, actions, and events, sequences of generation, individual cases, outcome distributions, transition metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. Scope of influence covers direct participants in generation, people taking up the work, indirectly affected people, second-order consequences, and impacts across domains and generations. Network topology scope covers propagation of conditions, collaboration among subjects, diffusion of events, and emergent connections. Scope of jurisdiction and authorization states the authorization for identification of generation, initiation of change, commitment of resources, and action concerning diffusion separately. State the scale separately for the generative condition, the generative subject, and the generative event. Interpretation: In addition to checking all nine axes separately, the generative condition, generative subject, and generative event each need their own stated scales; one overall scale must not conceal cross-level differences among conditions, actions, and consequences. [T-main]","17 | Effective object. Source text: A combination of conditions, subjects, and events that forms a detectable state transition. Interpretation: This describes the object corresponding to the variable; choosing a lower-threshold descriptive route does not permit treating functional meaning in the name as proven causality. [T-main]","18 | Kept across scales. Source text: Separation of the three categories of generative condition, generative subject, and generative event. Interpretation: What is kept across scales is a comparative responsibility to preserve during transfer, not a claim that variable values are unchanged at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Must be added when moving to a larger scale. Source text: New units and populations; relations of representation; kinds of responsibility; external impacts. Interpretation: Moving to a larger scale is not copying and enlarging the original table. When material about new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and other items is incomplete, limit conclusions that depend on them. [T-main]","20 | Changes with scale. Source text: Generative subjects, conditions, and events can change with scale. Interpretation: A word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; rebuild the correspondence rather than assume the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Objects to which it does not apply. Source text: Stable descriptions with neither the formation of a new state nor a claim about generation. Interpretation: The non-applicability restriction prevents unconditional projection of concepts concerning human norms, responsibility, and experience; it does not deny that other theories may study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Cannot be raised to. Source text: A condition or an event treated as a subject with intent. Interpretation: Inferences from local parts to wholes need separate proof; broad impact, grand names, and formal similarity cannot supply representativeness or authorization. [T-main]","23 | States. Source text: Potential; triggered; forming; interrupted; diffusing. Interpretation: States are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not maturity ladders for scoring people; state changes require time windows, grounds, and records of changes. [T-main]","24 | Observables. Source text: Records of when candidate conditions appear, change, or are removed; the generative subject’s capabilities, authorization, decisions, and actual actions; the pre-registered state transition from before to after the generative event; in emergence without a subject, records of the bridge between local interaction and aggregate outcome. Interpretation: Observable clues indicate where to find material; a single clue's existence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements. Source text: Records of initiation; windows for conditions; actions by subjects; mechanisms of interaction without a subject. Interpretation: Gather listed evidence according to route and task relevance. Distinguish unknown, not yet collected, not applicable, and withheld for protection; do not compel disclosure merely to fill the table. [T-main]","26 | Inputs. Source text: Orientation anchor (§7.3.3); resources and institutional conditions; conditions for causal claims. Interpretation: This states what enters from upstream and with what qualifications; gaps must not quietly disappear during transfer between variables. [T-main]","27 | Outputs. Source text: Need for uptake; state transitions; the start of a chain of responsibility. Interpretation: Outputs indicate which downstream judgments may depend on this variable; passing references does not guarantee downstream conclusions, actual repair, or authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag. Source text: State the accumulation of conditions, triggering events, and lags in formation. Interpretation: Occurrence, observation, execution, and the arrival of effects may happen at different times; short-term material cannot automatically certify extension beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty. Source text: Record joint generation, emergence without a subject, and subjects that cannot be identified. Interpretation: Locate uncertainty in specific material and positions; do not merely write an overall confidence score that conceals positions never covered at all. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone. Source text: Overlooked informal initiators and affected positions. Interpretation: An exclusion zone limits the coverage of the current inference; it does not erase these people or facts from the world. Material unavailable for safety reasons is neither consent nor evidence of no problem. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions. Source text: Initiators; people taking up the work; beneficiaries; people affected. Interpretation: Record both directly and indirectly affected people; visible members do not represent all cost bearers. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carriers. Source text: Initiators; procedures; technical facilities; relationship networks. Interpretation: A carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something. Its name, completed construction, and delivery of the required function require different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible parties. Source text: Those who actually act; decision makers; parties granting authorization. Interpretation: Specify who proposes a judgment and who operates or uses it; recording responsibility does not automatically constitute adjudication of legal liability or new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status. Source text: The fact of generation establishes neither normative justification nor a complete attribution of responsibility. Interpretation: Distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status as specified by the detailed description. Established facts do not independently determine value rankings, and normative reasons do not automatically establish empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling. Source text: When the mechanism chain is complete, judgments may go up to the level of explanation. Interpretation: A complete mechanism can support the explanatory level; route one still reaches only candidate classification, and route two likewise concerns a candidate mechanism for a specified channel. Neither grants responsibility assignments or continuing authorization. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling. Source text: This variable produces only candidate classifications by generative condition, generative subject, and generative event, descriptions of state transitions, and needs for more evidence. It does not authorize starting, spreading, or stopping a generative process. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass normative bridging (§4.14.1) and have in place the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion, itemized authorization, and operational procedures. Interpretation: The variable itself does not confer real-world authority to act; actual actions require separate checks of choice, normative bridging, explicitly stated normative premises, valid itemized authorization, operational procedures, protection, and validity periods. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples. Source text: A technological condition is incorrectly described as a generative subject with a goal; an emergent process without a unified initiator is forced into an attribution to one visible person. Interpretation: Use counterexamples to limit unconditional extrapolation. They may require narrowing the scope or obtaining new evidence, but do not automatically invalidate all bounded uses. [T-main]","38 | Appeal. Source text: Under the common rule for appeal and restoration (§7.4), people classified as generative subjects can raise objections to attributions of intent, role, and authorization through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection, and initiate a review independent of the original classification or decision chain. Interpretation: Appeals must be safely accessible, protected against retaliation, and capable of triggering independent review; a name alone does not imply authority to change errors. [T-main]","39 | Restoration. Source text: Under the same rule, once restoration is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline correct the categories of generative condition, generative subject, and generative event, and actually remove erroneous attributions of intent or responsibility and their downstream effects. Changes leave a record, and their completion is verified. Interpretation: Within valid authorization, actually revoke erroneous effects, correct downstream consequences, and verify completion; changing words in a document does not restore rights and resources to the person concerned. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Starting from a claim about a new state, record the generative condition, generative subject, generative event, and their sources separately.","Route one retains candidate status and unknown agents without personifying conditions.","If seeking a mechanism explanation, pre-register the specified state transition and the comparison of channel effects.","Check the scale, time window, and cross-level bridging for the generative condition, generative subject, and generative event separately.","Effects, normative justification, responsibility, and authorization must each be established; a generator does not automatically retain authority forever.","Disputed attributions may undergo independent review; after authorized correction, remove downstream effects."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Generative conditions","leftText":"Resource, technical, institutional, or situational conditions.","rightTitle":"Generative subjects","rightText":"Agents' capabilities, roles, and actions require separate checking."},{"leftTitle":"Generative events","leftText":"Detectable state transitions.","rightTitle":"Agent intention","rightText":"Known intent cannot be inferred backward from an outcome."},{"leftTitle":"Unidentified agent","leftText":"Currently unknown or unconfirmed.","rightTitle":"Emergence without an agent","rightText":"Requires mechanism material linking local interactions to an overall result."},{"leftTitle":"Fact of initiation","leftText":"An action was performed.","rightTitle":"Continuing authorization","rightText":"Its specified scope and validity period must be established independently."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the source text: a technological condition is incorrectly described as a generative subject with a goal.","Counterexample in the source text: an emergent process without a unified initiator is forced into an attribution to one visible person.","Teaching counterexample: someone speaks publicly first and is then recorded as the sole generator of every subsequent change.","Source boundary: this variable does not authorize initiating, spreading, or stopping generative processes."],"questions":["Can a crisis be described as intentional because it triggers change?","Does failure to find an initiator establish generation without an agent?","Does the existence of a generative condition prove that a generative event will occur?","Is changing the category label alone enough for correction?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/figures/hv04-types.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Conditions, actions, and events in Knight's technical chain","href":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Conditions, actions, and events in Knight's technical chain","text":"Sections 15–16 of the SEC's 2013 order record that one server was not updated in the 2012 deployment and that old code was triggered; §§19–21 record problems with messages and output controls.","sourceLabel":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release70694","sourceUrl":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","sourceLocator":"Order of 2013-10-16, §II, §§15–16 and 19–21, printed pages 1 and 6–7","boundary":"The regulatory order's bounded findings can serve as a classification comparator; they do not confer intent on a program, directly establish individual responsibility, or certify a channel-effect test under this framework.","href":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.4","start_line":4091,"end_line":4174,"startLine":4091,"endLine":4174}],"outputId":"section-a-4","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: three kinds of object within one new function","text":"A fictional platform opens a new interface, team A publishes a new service under existing permissions, and users form new exchange activities. The interface capability may be listed as a generative condition, team A as a candidate generative subject, its actual publishing action as evidence of agent action, and a specified change in exchange state as a candidate generative event. If users later combine spontaneously to form another state, not all subsequent developments can be attributed to A's original intent; a new object window and mechanism material are needed.","assumptions":"The platform, team, and events are constructed; no actual launch is involved.","boundary":"Candidate classification does not replace a channel-effect test or establish A's responsibility for, or authority over, all subsequent behavior."},{"title":"Teaching construction: not knowing who does not mean there was no one","text":"Constructed records show that queue rules changed at a particular time, but the initiation log is missing. Record a candidate event and an unknown agent, rather than ‘emergence without an agent has been proved.’ If a recovered log later shows that an authorized role changed the configuration, update the corresponding generative subject and leave a record of changes. If a mechanism of distributed interactions is found, its bridging and causal evidence also require separate checks.","assumptions":"A fictional record-recovery scenario.","boundary":"Retaining an unknown presupposes neither a hidden operator nor the absence of an agent; both assertions require evidence."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source text: a technological condition is incorrectly described as a generative subject with a goal.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source text: an emergent process without a unified initiator is forced into an attribution to one visible person.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: someone speaks publicly first and is then recorded as the sole generator of every subsequent change.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: this variable does not authorize initiating, spreading, or stopping generative processes.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can a crisis be described as intentional because it triggers change?","answer":"No; it may be a condition or an event, while agent intent requires separate checking.","basis":"“Cannot infer.”"},{"question":"Does failure to find an initiator establish generation without an agent?","answer":"No; distinguish an unidentified agent from a supported mechanism without an agent.","basis":"Conditions for suspension and routes one and two."},{"question":"Does the existence of a generative condition prove that a generative event will occur?","answer":"No; the existence of one category under route one does not prove the other two or a generative mechanism.","basis":"The ceiling of route one."},{"question":"Is changing the category label alone enough for correction?","answer":"Erroneous attributions of intent or responsibility and their downstream effects must be revoked, and completion verified.","basis":"The “Restoration” item."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/figures/hv04-types.png","alt":"Record generative conditions, generative subjects, and generative events separately; an unidentified agent is not proof of the absence of an agent.","caption":"Based on the three-way distinction and pause conditions in A.4."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/figures/hv04-routes.png","alt":"Route one: candidate classification; route two: effects on specified state transitions supported by a pre-registered channel-effect test; facts of generation do not confer normative justification, responsibility, or authorization.","caption":"Based on the two complete routes in A.4."}],"diagramBrief":"The first diagram separates generative conditions, generative subjects, and generative events; the second distinguishes classification and mechanism routes. A blank agent remains unknown; arrows do not create intent or responsibility.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["There is no actual channel-effect test; this topic explains only classification and eligibility for mechanism claims.","Unidentified agents, emergence without an agent, joint generation, and legal liability all require proportionate independent evidence; visibility does not determine them."],"taskId":"S-A-4","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.4, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 3766–3831","claim_support":["All 39 fields of HV04 and its generation-classification/mechanism routes."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-types","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§7.2–7.2.3, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 1486–1501","claim_support":["Definitions of the strong types GC, GS, and GE and the prohibition on mixing them."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-G2","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.4, Chinese source lines 610–619","claim_support":["Specified channel effects and dimension-by-dimension boundedness."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-KNIGHT","title":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release70694","url":"https://www.sec.gov/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013-10-16","locator":"Order of 2013-10-16, §II, §§15–16 and 19–21, printed pages 1 and 6–7","claim_support":["The specific technical chain involving a deployment omission, triggering of old code, and output controls; the document contains findings in a settlement order."],"limits":["The author read the order locally in part; except as to jurisdiction, the respondent neither admitted nor denied the findings. The technical chain is not used to confer intent on machines, automatically assign individual responsibility, or masquerade as a G2 instance."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"A.4　生成节点（HV04）; Chinese source lines 4034–4117; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/source/S-A-4.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"e5cf2d13b553abde931f9474292328e667c8ac2c9604e737738d36ebcca6a68b","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-04.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-04.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-04.md"};function l(u,e,h,p,g,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Generative Node (HV04) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.4 Generative Node (HV04) {#section-a-4}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">The variable “Generative Node” separates generative conditions, actors, and events. An unidentified actor does not mean an absence of actors has been proved; the fact of generation creates neither justification, complete responsibility, nor continuing authority.</span></div><ol><li>Proposition and Scope of Application</li></ol><p>Proposition: Generation must be described as three separate items: the generative condition (§7.2.1), the generative subject (§7.2.2), and the generative event (§7.2.3). Emergent generation can occur without an identifiable subject.</p><p>Scope of application: Formation of new actions, organizations, institutions, or state transitions in human structures.</p><p>Conditions for suspension: Suspend the relevant claims about the subject or the type when a condition is personified, an event is treated as a subject, or standing as a subject has not been confirmed on any grounds. Route one may still record the candidate generative condition, generative subject, and generative event separately, along with the status “subject not yet identified.” A subject not yet identified is not taken as proof that there is none.</p><ol start="2"><li>Grounds and Limits on Conclusions</li></ol><p>Inferential premises: None.</p><p>Method requirements: Conditions for evidence (§2.5); conditions for sources (§2.7).</p><p>Domain restriction: None.</p><p>Objects of application: None.</p><p>Support routes:</p><p>Route one, sorting into categories. Conclusion level: Descriptive classification. Conditions: The items required for the generative condition, the generative subject, and the generative event can each be stated separately, and the two statuses “emergence without a subject” and “subject not yet identified” are retained. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Record a candidate generative condition, a candidate generative subject, and a candidate generative event separately. The three do not substitute for one another. Ceiling: Distinction by category only. The existence of a condition, subject, or event does not establish either of the other two or a causal mechanism of generation.</p><p>Route two, generative mechanism. Conclusion level: Mechanism explanation. Conditions: A pre-registered channel-effect test (§4.4) identifies an above-threshold effect of the generative condition, the generative subject, or a channel of emergence without a subject on the state transition of the specified generative event. Further inferential premises: This channel-effect test. Further method requirements: Conditions for causal claims (§2.4); Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals (§4.8.4). Can conclude: Record a candidate mechanism of generation within the specified scale, time window, and channel, and its classification by generative condition, generative subject, and generative event. Ceiling: Conditions must not be personified, a subject must not be inferred backward from an event, and normative justification, responsibility, or authorization must not be inferred from the fact of generation.</p><p>Can infer: Conditional paths of generation; generation with or without a subject.</p><p>Cannot infer: That a technology or a crisis has intent; that a generative subject automatically has continuing authorization.</p><ol start="3"><li>Scale and Object</li></ol><p>Nine-axis scale: Aggregation level covers units of exposure to conditions, of action by subjects, and of generative events, with their respective populations of cases, distributions, and aggregation rules. Spatial extent covers sites of generation, organizational or platform boundaries, areas of diffusion, and cross-domain environments. Time span covers the period of accumulation of conditions, the window of the subject’s action, the time of the event, and diffusion lag. Organizational level covers roles in action, generative teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. Causal level covers generative events, mechanisms of interaction between subjects and conditions, meso-level structures of generation, institutional arrangements, and systemic conditions. Observation resolution covers the original conditions, actions, and events, sequences of generation, individual cases, outcome distributions, transition metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. Scope of influence covers direct participants in generation, people taking up the work, indirectly affected people, second-order consequences, and impacts across domains and generations. Network topology scope covers propagation of conditions, collaboration among subjects, diffusion of events, and emergent connections. Scope of jurisdiction and authorization states the authorization for identification of generation, initiation of change, commitment of resources, and action concerning diffusion separately. State the scale separately for the generative condition, the generative subject, and the generative event.</p><p>Effective object: A combination of conditions, subjects, and events that forms a detectable state transition.</p><p>Kept across scales: Separation of the three categories of generative condition, generative subject, and generative event.</p><p>Must be added when moving to a larger scale: New units and populations; relations of representation; kinds of responsibility; external impacts.</p><p>Changes with scale: Generative subjects, conditions, and events can change with scale.</p><p>Objects to which it does not apply: Stable descriptions with neither the formation of a new state nor a claim about generation.</p><p>Cannot be raised to: A condition or an event treated as a subject with intent.</p><ol start="4"><li>State, Evidence, and Relations Between Variables</li></ol><p>States: Potential; triggered; forming; interrupted; diffusing.</p><p>Observables: Records of when candidate conditions appear, change, or are removed; the generative subject’s capabilities, authorization, decisions, and actual actions; the pre-registered state transition from before to after the generative event; in emergence without a subject, records of the bridge between local interaction and aggregate outcome.</p><p>Evidence requirements: Records of initiation; windows for conditions; actions by subjects; mechanisms of interaction without a subject.</p><p>Inputs: Orientation anchor (§7.3.3); resources and institutional conditions; conditions for causal claims.</p><p>Outputs: Need for uptake; state transitions; the start of a chain of responsibility.</p><p>Time window and lag: State the accumulation of conditions, triggering events, and lags in formation.</p><p>Uncertainty: Record joint generation, emergence without a subject, and subjects that cannot be identified.</p><p>Local exclusion zone: Overlooked informal initiators and affected positions.</p><p>Affected positions: Initiators; people taking up the work; beneficiaries; people affected.</p><ol start="5"><li>Uptake, Responsibility, Ceilings, and Correction</li></ol><p>Uptake carriers: Initiators; procedures; technical facilities; relationship networks.</p><p>Responsible parties: Those who actually act; decision makers; parties granting authorization.</p><p>Normative status: The fact of generation establishes neither normative justification nor a complete attribution of responsibility.</p><p>Judgment ceiling: When the mechanism chain is complete, judgments may go up to the level of explanation.</p><p>Action ceiling: This variable produces only candidate classifications by generative condition, generative subject, and generative event, descriptions of state transitions, and needs for more evidence. It does not authorize starting, spreading, or stopping a generative process. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass normative bridging (§4.14.1) and have in place the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion, itemized authorization, and operational procedures.</p><p>Counterexamples: A technological condition is incorrectly described as a generative subject with a goal; an emergent process without a unified initiator is forced into an attribution to one visible person.</p><p>Appeal: Under the common rule for appeal and restoration (§7.4), people classified as generative subjects can raise objections to attributions of intent, role, and authorization through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection, and initiate a review independent of the original classification or decision chain.</p><p>Restoration: Under the same rule, once restoration is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline correct the categories of generative condition, generative subject, and generative event, and actually remove erroneous attributions of intent or responsibility and their downstream effects. Changes leave a record, and their completion is verified.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"A.4"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=n(d,[["render",l]]);export{v as __pageData,b as default};
